{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475776-question-chinese-and-japanese-names-and-written-characters","id":1475776,"title":"Question: Chinese and Japanese names and written characters","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-03-04T08:25:57-08:00","id":1475776,"title":"Question: Chinese and Japanese names and written characters","content":"Hello, is anyone on this forum familiar with Chinese, Japanese, or Korean characters?<br /> <br /> Years ago, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23\" data-vbnamecard=\"23\">Saur Ops Specialist</a> posted a <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1418-wta-and-cjvk-chaaracter-writing\">transliteration (and correction) of Chinese and Japanese</a> names and terms from Werewolf into the characters of those written languages.<br /> <br /> I would like to know of anyone has figured out the correct characters for Chinese, Japanese, and Korean (and Vietnamese?) names in Mage? Just the names of the many Mage and Sorcerer groups from those cultures (Li-Hai, Lin Shen, Mo-Tzu Fa, Wu Shan, Shi-Ren, Gam Lung, Han Fei Tzu, Hei Guan, Kaizankai, Tenshi Arashi Ryu, Hong Lei, K\u2019an Lu, Dalou\u2019laoshi, Taiping Tianguo, Zi Guang, Miao Guan, Jyu-Kin, Mirainohmen, Tai Hoi Li, Wu-Keng, Nung Wa, Wu Nung, Jidai, Wulong, Shih 'hunters').<br /> <br /> I tried to take a stab at Go Kamisori Gama and Zaibatsu using Wiktionary and came up with \u4f0d\u5243\u5200\u938c and \u8ca1\u95a5, but I don't know if that's correct.<br /> <br /> Could anyone help me with this please? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":896,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","user":"Erinys","date":". 03-05-2022, 01:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-04T16:20:51-08:00","id":1475808,"title":"","content":"There were Korean names in MtAs?<br /> <br /> EDIT: Argh, forum ate my post.<br /> I recognize Hyemyeong-seunim at least. Should be spelled as \ud61c\uba85\uc2a4\ub2d8 in Korean.<br /> \ud61c hey \uba85 myeong \uc2a4 seu \ub2d8 nim.<br /> \u2018Seunim\u2019 is just the (respectful) word for a Buddhist monk. It\u2019s a \u2018pure\u2019 Korean word, so no hanja here.<br /> \u2018Hyemyeong\u2019 could mean anything depending on the hanja used, but as a high-ranking Akashic, I suspect it was \u6167\u660e, literally meaning Wise Brightness, which was also the Buddhist-name of a real-life historical monk back in late Joseon.<br /> Or possibly \u6167\u547d, meaning Wise Calling, a word meaning either the continuation of Buddhist legacies and teachings, or enlightened wisdom. Rarely used outside of actual Buddhists.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":12619,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","user":"21C Hermit","date":". 03-04-2022, 07:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-05T10:39:13-08:00","id":1475846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475776-question-chinese-and-japanese-names-and-written-characters?p=1475808#post1475808\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There were Korean names in MtAs?</div> </div> </div> </div>I have no idea?<br /> Wiktionary says that some Chinese/CJK Unified characters are legible in Korean and/or Vietnamese. Everything I know about these characters (which is almost nothing) I learned on Wiktionary.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":896,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","user":"Erinys","date":". 03-06-2022, 09:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-06T10:28:27-08:00","id":1475911,"title":"","content":"Japanese is complicated. Kana are easy and purely phonetic, but Kanji are used in several, interchangeable ways, as they may be used either for one of their sounds or one of their direct meanings.<br /> <br /> And this complication is important, because they use it a lot for word-plays, <b>especially</b> in names. So even two people with the same name in both sound and meaning may have it written with distinct Kanji because of the word-play their parents wanted. Like-wise, two people with the same or almost the same written name can actually have completely different names in meaning and sound.<br /> <br /> If you just want an approximation that is nice for people who don't speak Japanese, then almost anything works. If you want to really make native equivalents for the names that are Japanese, then you'll need to parse and discuss every single name with a Japanese speaker (and my Japanese is still too weak for that).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-06T18:09:18-08:00","id":1475956,"title":"","content":"Don't any of these these names have English translations that would help? Gam Lung are also called the Golden Dragon Society, right?<br /> <br /> I checked Wiktionary and it says Saengsaeng is Korean.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-06T18:42:06-08:00","id":1475958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475776-question-chinese-and-japanese-names-and-written-characters?p=1475956#post1475956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I checked Wiktionary and it says Saengsaeng is Korean.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u2026 Really? My search doesn\u2019t come up with anything. And as a Korean (not just of descent, but living here right now), I have no idea what that word\u2019s supposed to mean.<br /> <br /> Closest I suspect is \u2018seonsaeng\u2019 (\uc120\uc0dd; \uc120 seon \uc0dd saeng) which means usually means \u2018teacher\u2019 and sometimes \u2018mister\u2019 or \u2018sir\u2019. (It\u2019s actually the same word as the Japanese \u2018sensei\u2019, albeit with different pronunciation and slightly different context)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-07T01:00:59-08:00","id":1475973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475776-question-chinese-and-japanese-names-and-written-characters?p=1475956#post1475956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't any of these these names have English translations that would help? Gam Lung are also called the Golden Dragon Society, right?<br /> <br /> I checked Wiktionary and it says Saengsaeng is Korean.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem if you're going for their native scripts is that those scripts do not work like that, period. So it is not just a matter of translating to help.<br /> <br /> Giving a simple example for Japanese, the character Light in Death Note. His name is indeed Light, in English and with the obvious meaning. The pronunciation is actually &quot;Raito&quot;, but just because this is how most Japanese will pronounce it.<br /> <br /> Finally, that's a lot of ways for you to write it in Japanese. As a foreign word, the technically correct way to do so would be to use Katakana, so it would be a phonetic approximation (and they prefer to use Hiragana for proper Japanese, despite doing the same job). But his mother specifically chose, among many options, to write his name with the Kanji for Moon.<br /> <br /> You see, his name isn't Moon. His name does not means Moon. His name isn't even Japanese. The Kanji is there only to signify &quot;light&quot;, with another appended Kanji to correct the pronunciation. A Japanese reading it will either read &quot;Light&quot; or be confused and ask how it should be pronounced. But her specific choice of Kanji conveys something that isn't in the spoken name and wouldn't be on a translation. There is no direct translation for &quot;Light, but written as if it was Moon&quot;. This is a word-play that only exists in written Japanese.<br /> <br /> Chinese and Korean have similar, and at the same time widely distinct practices at play. Traditional Chinese has no direct phonetic symbols at all, that's why bad translations from Chinese characters to English are so weird. Everything is constructed out of pure ideograms, so when you write anything at all it isn't a representation of a sound, it is a combination of words to form a neologism. In practice, the written form of Chinese is its own language that can't be transliterated into English because it lacks sounds entirely (this is on purpose, as the written form was devised to facilitate communication through language barriers).<br /> <br /> Korean has its own phonetic system, but also borrows and adapt Chinese ideograms in a way neither the Chinese or Japanese do. I can't say much in detail, and certainly not even close to how much an actual Korean can, so I'll let <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12619_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12619\" data-vbnamecard=\"12619\">21C Hermit</a> fill us in, but you'll have again a lot of variations depending on how the Hanja are used, or even if the writer prefers Hanja or Hangul depending on the word/name and time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-07T02:13:31-08:00","id":1475977,"title":"","content":"Not sure what I could fill in here, though I\u2019d love to. I\u2019m not good at giving abstract/generalized briefings. Gimme a specific example, then I fare better.<br /> <br /> For now I can say that all words are written in Hangeul/Hangul like \u2018\uc120\uc0dd\u2019 for \u2018seonsaeng\u2019, but dig in deeper and you\u2019ll see that a LOT of Korean words (especially nouns) are from directly reading Chinese ideograms with Korean pronunciation. So \uc120\uc0dd is actually \u5148\u751f, which literally means \u2018born earlier\u2019, from which we derive the meaning of \u2018teacher\u2019. Of course, there are \u2018pure\u2019 Korean words like \u2018balam/baram\u2019, \ubc14\ub78c, which means \u2018wind\u2019. (Oh, but older and/or academic texts tend to flat-out write the original Chinese ideograms <i>without</i> writing them in Hangeul whatsoever, because such \u2018mixed writing\u2019 used to be the norm in the past. Give such a text to a modern-day Korean who didn\u2019t major in the relevant field though, and he\u2019ll likely shiver and run away <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> )","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-07T07:55:50-08:00","id":1475984,"title":"","content":"The forum deleted my post <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475776-question-chinese-and-japanese-names-and-written-characters?p=1475958#post1475958\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2026 Really? My search doesn\u2019t come up with anything. And as a Korean (not just of descent, but living here right now), I have no idea what that word\u2019s supposed to mean.<br /> <br /> Closest I suspect is \u2018seonsaeng\u2019 (\uc120\uc0dd; \uc120 seon \uc0dd saeng) which means usually means \u2018teacher\u2019 and sometimes \u2018mister\u2019 or \u2018sir\u2019. (It\u2019s actually the same word as the Japanese \u2018sensei\u2019, albeit with different pronunciation and slightly different context)</div> </div> </div> </div>Then Wiktionary must be wrong <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Or whoever came up with that name made a mistake.<br /> <br /> I'm not asking &quot;what is the canon writing&quot; since apparently those weren't provided. I'm not asking &quot;what do they actually write in the WoD&quot; since they aren't real people. I'm not asking &quot;what is every possible way each name could possibly be written&quot; (that would be absurd!)<br /> <br /> I'm only asking &quot;what is <i>an </i><i><i>a</i>ppropriate</i> writing&quot; that fits the meaning without being stupid or nonsensical. I don't know if Saur Ops Specialist is Chinese or Japanese or neither, but they had the translations and contexts for the names they transliterated and explained how they avoided nonsensical characters.<br /> <br /> <br /> Edit: for the example named Light, proposing a character that means 'light' (the visual phenomenon) would still make sense, because there's no &quot;true&quot; canonical nor real-world spelling to search for by detective work. Proposing a character that means 'light-weight' or 'low-fat yoghurt' would not make sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":896,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","user":"Erinys","date":". 03-07-2022, 07:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-07T20:09:02-08:00","id":1476020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475776-question-chinese-and-japanese-names-and-written-characters?p=1475984#post1475984\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Proposing a character that means 'light-weight' or 'low-fat yoghurt' would not make sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>In Japanese something on those lines could actually happen, again depending on what they want to convey. If it is possible, believe, some father in some corner of Japan did named his son &quot;Light&quot; with the kanji for &quot;low-fat yogurt&quot;. The actual Japanese traditions on naming aren't anything like most people in the Americas and Europe believe they are.<br /> <br /> But that aside, the whole point stands that you need actual speakers to decide on an adequate writing, so I'm not qualified. If the chosen symbols have no reason beyond conveying the sound, they already failed to seem authentic, so it would have to be something that serves the purpose and context of the word on the setting.<br /> <br /> \u200bOh, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12619_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12619\" data-vbnamecard=\"12619\">21C Hermit</a>, saengsaeng may not be Korean, but it approximates Korean more than the others. And on a quick Google search I found two business named something similar and owned by Koreans, a noodle called saeng saeng and a Korean owned <i>Brazilian </i>engineering company called Saeng Engenharia. That's nothing but a curio.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-07T20:43:24-08:00","id":1476022,"title":"","content":"There is\u2026 one thing saeng-saeng could mean; literally Life-Life. But it usually means \u2018super fresh\u2019, and that\u2019s likely what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> saw.<br /> <br /> And it\u2019s a really weird name for a group of Technocrats in that sense, answering <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar896_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"896\" data-vbnamecard=\"896\">Erinys</a> question of \u201cwhat is the appropriate writing\u201d with the answer of Nope.<br /> <br /> Unless, of course, it\u2019s a garbled \u2018seonsaeng\u2019. But I\u2019ve looked up on those guys, and with their Japanese bias, it feels\u2026 like\u2026 I dunno, a chapter of the modern-day Thule Society calling themselves the Qabbalah or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-08T05:52:48-08:00","id":1476040,"title":"","content":"Only now I looked who they were... yeah...<br /> <br /> Unless someone in the writing team got hold of a really old usage of the term, I tend to agree that it doesn't fit then in any sense at all. First of all, why even Korean in particular? Is there anything in their history to justify this? It seems that languages were chosen at random as if it didn't made as much a difference in Asia as it does in Europe...<br /> <br /> But even then, given who they are, I think the name would probably be written entirely in Traditional Chinese or, if really Korean, in Hanja as a tribute to traditional literature (that was primarily in Chinese script/Hanja in Korea up to the early XIX century by what I've learned, could be wrong). If their Japanese bias is old enough it would make more sense to stir away from Korean entirely, though, unless they have an extremely specific history to justify it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}